## WHAT YOU MUST FIX — NO CODE, UNDERSTAND THE APPROACH

### FIX 1 — Two Completely Separate Code Paths

Right now init and incremental indexing share the same code. That is the root mistake.

Split them into two entirely different paths that never share logic:

**Path A — First Run (init)**
Full scan. Expected to be slow. User is told this upfront. Parallelise parsing as hard as possible across all CPU cores. Wiki generation does NOT run during this path — it queues for background. When structural indexing finishes, record the current Git commit hash as the snapshot baseline. Done.

**Path B — Every Run After (incremental)**
Never scan the filesystem. Never hash a file. Ask Git what changed between the stored commit hash and HEAD. Process exactly those files and nothing else. Update the stored commit hash. Done.

These two paths must be completely separate functions. No shared loop. No shared scanner. No shared anything except the storage layer they write to.

---

### FIX 2 — Git Is the Change Detector, Not the File System

Remove all file hashing from the incremental path entirely.

Git already hashed every file. Every tracked file has a blob OID. If the blob OID in Git's index matches the blob OID stored in Synapse's database, that file has not changed. This is mathematically guaranteed. You do not need to verify it.

The incremental path should ask Git one question: what files changed between commit X and commit Y? Git answers with a list of paths. Process that list. Nothing else.

This eliminates the single biggest I/O bottleneck — reading every file on every run just to check if it changed.

---

### FIX 3 — Separate the Deterministic and Non-Deterministic Pipelines Forever

Structural indexing (parsing, symbols, edges) is deterministic and fast.
Wiki generation (LLM calls) is non-deterministic and slow.

These must never be in the same loop again.

Structural indexing completes. Then it puts work into a queue. It does not wait. It does not check on that work. It does not care when it finishes.

Wiki generation runs as a completely separate background worker. It reads from the queue. It processes when it can. If it fails, it retries. If the LLM is down, structural indexing still works perfectly.

The user sees structural indexing finish fast. Wiki updates appear over time in the background. This is the correct UX.

---

### FIX 4 — The Index Is a Snapshot, Not a Live View

Every time indexing completes, record exactly which Git commit hash the index represents.

This single stored value unlocks everything:

- **Incremental detection** — compare stored hash to HEAD, ask Git for the delta
- **Rollback** — restore index to any previous commit's state
- **Branch switching** — each branch stores its own last-indexed commit hash
- **Consistency guarantee** — the index always represents exactly one commit, never a partial state
- **Recovery** — if the index is corrupted, replay from stored commit hashes in Git history

Without this stored hash, Synapse cannot know what it knows. With it, Synapse always knows exactly where it stands.

---

### FIX 5 — Fix the Database Write Pattern

The database layer has three problems. Fix all three:

**Problem A — One transaction per edge**
Every edge currently gets its own database commit. A project with 5,000 edges is doing 5,000 disk syncs. All edges from a single indexing run must go into one transaction. Collect everything, write once.

**Problem B — One insert per symbol**
Every symbol currently gets its own INSERT call. Use batch inserts. One call for all symbols from a file. One call for all files in a batch. SQLite is extremely fast when you use it this way and extremely slow when you don't.

**Problem C — Suffix matching with LIKE**
Module resolution is currently doing WHERE path LIKE "%something" which cannot use any index and scans every row. Store the importable module name as its own indexed column when the file is first indexed. Then resolution is a single indexed lookup, not a full table scan.

---

### FIX 6 — Fix Symbol Search

Symbol name search is currently doing LIKE "%name%" which scans every symbol in the database.

SQLite ships with FTS5 full-text search built in. Use it. It was designed exactly for this. No external dependency. No extra installation. Already in SQLite.

---

### FIX 7 — Read Each File Exactly Once

Currently the same file content is read three times — once for hashing, once for parsing, once for wiki generation.

Read it once. Store the content in memory for the duration of that file's processing. Pass it to every stage that needs it. Nothing reads the disk again for that file.

---

### FIX 8 — Wiki Is a Lazy Cache, Not an Eager Build

Do not generate wiki for every file at index time. Most files in a repository are never asked about.

Wiki pages are generated when something requests them and cached until the underlying file changes. When the file changes, the wiki page is marked stale. It is regenerated on the next request, not immediately.

This means wiki generation cost is proportional to what agents actually use, not the size of the repository.

---

### FIX 9 — First Run Must Parallelise Parsing

First run is the only time you must process every file. Make it as fast as possible.

Parsing is CPU-bound. Run it across all available CPU cores simultaneously. The critical constraint is that Tree-sitter parsers are not thread-safe — each worker must have its own parser instance. Use process-based parallelism, not thread-based.

Show the user real progress numbers during first run. How many files. How many symbols. How long elapsed. What it costs. Make it feel like infrastructure being built.

---

### FIX 10 — Memory Does Not Accumulate

Currently all parse results for all changed files are held in memory until the entire pass completes. For a large first run this causes memory pressure.

Process in batches. Write each batch to the database immediately. Release the memory. The next batch starts with a clean slate. Memory usage stays flat regardless of repository size.

