## SKILLS — APPLY ALL OF THESE

### Git Mastery
- Use git diff-tree to get changed files between two commits — this is the correct tool
- Use git ls-tree to get blob OIDs for all tracked files — this is O(1) per file
- Use git's own commit hash as your change detection mechanism — it is already correct
- Never trust the filesystem more than you trust Git
- Understand that a Git commit is an immutable snapshot — model your index the same way

### SQLite Performance
- WAL mode must always be on — enables concurrent reads during writes
- PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL during bulk loads — safe with WAL, much faster
- Always use batch inserts — executemany is an order of magnitude faster than a loop
- Always wrap related writes in one transaction — never commit per row
- FTS5 for any text search — it exists, it is fast, use it
- Index every column you filter or join on — check with EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN
- PRAGMA integrity_check on startup — catch corruption before it causes wrong results
- Never use LIKE with a leading wildcard — it cannot use indexes

### Concurrency
- CPU-bound work (parsing) — use process-based parallelism, not threads
- I/O-bound work (LLM calls) — use async
- Never mix CPU-bound and I/O-bound in the same executor
- Tree-sitter is not thread-safe — one parser instance per process, never shared
- Use a queue to decouple fast work (structural) from slow work (LLM)
- A queue means the producer never waits for the consumer

### Architecture
- Separate every code path that has a different speed — fast and slow must never share a loop
- Event sourcing: treat commits as events, index as accumulated state, always replayable
- Lazy evaluation: generate expensive things only when asked, cache the result
- Fail fast: validate everything at startup, never discover problems mid-operation
- One source of truth: Git is the truth, the index is a derived projection

### Testing
- Test first run on an empty database — must complete without errors
- Test incremental run with zero changes — must complete in under 100ms
- Test incremental run with one change — must only process that one file
- Test with two files that have identical content — must not crash (file_id bug)
- Test rollback to previous commit — index must match that commit exactly
- Test branch switch with active agent — must warn, must checkpoint, must switch correctly
- Test every CLI command — not just happy path, test error cases too
- Test with no LLM configured — every structural feature must still work
- Run EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN on every database query — no full table scans allowed

### CLI Quality
- Every command must produce output — nothing runs silently
- Every destructive command asks for confirmation
- Progress must show real numbers — files processed, time elapsed, cost so far
- Errors must say exactly what failed and exactly how to fix it
- Every command must have --help that explains what it touches on disk
- Commands must work in any order — no hidden dependency on previous commands having run

### Documentation
- README must explain what Synapse is in one paragraph with no buzzwords
- Every architectural decision must have a one-line reason next to it in the docs
- The performance architecture must be documented — why two paths, why git delta
- Release notes must list every change with its user-visible impact

